WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.040
- Please join me in welcoming Barbara Grosz.

00:00:11.569 --> 00:00:14.741
- Thanks very much for that
wonderful introduction.

00:00:14.741 --> 00:00:14.772
- Thanks very much for that
wonderful introduction.
It's really a great honor to be here
giving a keynote at Faculty Summit

00:00:14.772 --> 00:00:25.080
It's really a great honor to be here
giving a keynote at Faculty Summit

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:31.660
where the theme is focused on AI at the Edge.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:36.180
It's a time where it's really important
for those of us doing research in AI

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.660
to think about what
capabilities systems have,

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.280
what our methods can do,
what they can't do,

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.840
where the challenges are

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.880
and what we may be
doing in the future.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:50.230
And for everyone in computer science
to think about how we can design systems

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:53.040
that actually do good in the world,

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.040
and don't do harm.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:02.820
This is perhaps, this is Turing's
best-known conjecture

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.390
about building machines
with intelligence.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.010
And by the way he equated
intelligence with thinking.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:13.470
He thought they were, he wasn't talking
about intelligence as being able to do research

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:14.770
that gets a Nobel Prize.

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:18.470
So his most well-known conjectures
are about what he called

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:20.470
being able
to participate

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:22.190
well in the imitation game,

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:24.490
now talked about
as passing the Turing test.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:32.100
That conjecture, that we would have machines
able to do that by the year 2000 was not realized.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.800
But the conjecture that you see on this slide was.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.060
People now regularly talk about machines
as though they were thinking.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:42.160
Of course, they frequently also ask
what were they thinking,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.110
when they do things
that don't make sense.

00:01:44.730 --> 00:01:51.230
So about five years ago, when we were celebrating
the 100th anniversary of Turing's birth,

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.800
I was asked to give a talk
at a Turing symposium,

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.720
and I was trying to figure out

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.410
what I could say that was novel,

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.910
because people have talked about
Turing and written about Turing.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:06.400
And I decided it would be interesting to think
about if Turing were with us now,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:08.400
I mean there are people
who live to 100 years.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:16.040
If he were with us now, what would he be
Just to the people watching the clock.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:17.300
This clock is wrong.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.120
I am not four minutes over.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:24.510
So you might want to reset it.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:26.750
OK.

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.750
They didn't hear me.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.970
I'll try to keep to my time without the clock.

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:38.160
OK so, and of course no one knows
what Turing would say.

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.340
They got it.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:49.490
But I thought the world had changed a lot
and I wanted to put a challenge to the AI community.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.410
I hoped that other people would also do that,
so I still welcome other challenges.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:56.700
And this is the challenge that I came up with.

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:00.920
And it's really important to notice
that this is about working

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.090
as a team member over the long term,
not one-shot deals;

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:05.870
not just one question,
one answer,

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:07.640
we can do that just fine.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:13.840
And I also want to say, it says here that
you won't notice that it's not human.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:18.820
It's not to say that the systems would be
indistinguishable from humans,

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.290
it's fine to know you're working
with a computer system.

00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:23.780
But it has to behave sensibly.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.150
So I'll give you an example from healthcare.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:31.340
A smart partner that searches documents much faster
than any human being could do,

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:36.020
if it delivers sensible results,
passes the test.

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:38.040
If it delivers nonsense it doesn't.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:41.040
OK why now?

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.880
Because AI has really
made it out into the world.

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:49.260
The focus in this meeting, the descriptions
of the focus of this summit

00:03:49.260 --> 00:03:52.570
all make it very clear

00:03:52.570 --> 00:03:56.630
that we now have AI
capabilities entering daily life.

00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:02.140
So we really ought to think about how the systems
we're building are going to work with people.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.900
Another thing that's happened,
as AI technologies have made it into the world,

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.400
we see widespread investments in AI,

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:13.130
we see lots of claims about them.

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:17.450
We see lots of fantasies, not only
that you can let your car drive

00:04:17.450 --> 00:04:19.300
while you play games or watch movies.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.070
Don't do it.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:22.080
We're not there yet.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.490
But also in healthcare, in elder care, in education.

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:31.380
And it is crucial as we think about this
to think not simply about the systems,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.480
but about the people
that they interact with.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:36.910
And of course, we also have a lot of nightmares.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:41.930
I will say the nightmares are not new.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.610
People have been thinking about building machines,
could a human being build a machine,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.900
create something that could think?

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:51.390
That was like a person?

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:52.490
For centuries.

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:55.410
It goes back at least
to the Golem of Prague.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.290
Certainly to Frankenstein.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.920
And more recently we have things
like the movie Ex Machina.

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:07.230
So on the one hand, this android dream
set a context for Turing's conjecture.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:12.530
On the other hand, it makes clear
that there has, at least in western culture,

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.690
it's very different in Japan, by the way,
they think of robots as cuddly.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.720
And I'll leave it as a question for you
to think about the cultural part.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:29.190
So my argument is, if we want to get away
from the philosophical focus,

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:31.980
and Turing was asking
a philosophical question.

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:38.940
And away from the science fiction
of machines gone mad to get real intelligent behavior,

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:42.290
we need to think
about building machines

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:44.420
that augment our capabilities.

00:05:44.420 --> 00:05:47.760
Not that replace us
or replicate human intelligence.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:49.440
So here's my quip.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:52.070
We know how to replicate
human intelligence.

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.000
The process is known
to be fun.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:56.900
The product is babies.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:00.750
Even when they grow up,
they have only human intelligence.

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:05.050
We don't need to build machines
that have more of that, we need to build machines

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.370
that complement that.

00:06:06.370 --> 00:06:09.730
And I'm going to very quickly
talk about five reasons

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:14.000
why we want to aim
for coordination and teamwork now.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:17.520
The first two are about how
times have changed since Turing.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.360
Then about our responsibilities.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.510
And then because we're doing research,
we want to do exciting research.

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.880
So that's how people use computers.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:28.300
In Turing's day, one person, one machine.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:33.300
Now we have many people,
in many places, in many systems, working at many times.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.720
There are several arguments
from the cognitive sciences,

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:40.110
I'll just say
a couple briefly.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:42.550
Brain development.

00:06:43.190 --> 00:06:48.070
We know that in the first
two years of life if you don't have social interaction,

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:52.180
your brain is smaller
and it generates less energy.

00:06:52.180 --> 00:06:57.290
And you're basically cooked for life if you don't have
that social interaction in time.

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:03.380
We also know that language
learning depends on interpersonal interaction.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:09.000
There's some wonderful work
by Patricia Kuhl at the University of Washington.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.400
I'll give you one example.

00:07:11.370 --> 00:07:17.640
She looked at babies, American babies,
no exposure to Mandarin.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.020
Looking at the issue, so we're all born,
for those who don't know,

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:25.750
we're all born able
to recognize the phonemes

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:27.500
of all languages in the world.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:32.100
By the time we're 12 months old,
we know only the phonemes of the languages

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:34.060
that we've been exposed

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.080
to in those first 12 months.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.590
Somewhere between eight
and 10 months of age,

00:07:39.690 --> 00:07:41.860
English-speaking babies
understand the difference

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.550
between L and R,
and Japanese-speaking babies

00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:46.160
have lost that distinction.

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:48.520
Many other examples.

00:07:48.520 --> 00:07:54.390
She compared exposing babies
between 8 and 10 months to American babies,

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.590
to a speaker of Mandarin

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.750
face-to-face and watching
that same person on video.

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:05.110
The Mandarin phonemes were retained by the babies
who had the face-to-face interaction,

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.810
not by the ones
watching the video.

00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:09.600
OK what about engineering?

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:14.130
Well, if we want to build good systems,
we have to think about the people

00:08:14.130 --> 00:08:15.460
that we're building systems for

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:21.130
and how their different
cognitive processes work.

00:08:21.130 --> 00:08:24.420
I'll come back to language later
in the talk, but here's an example

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:28.010
to illustrate getting things wrong.

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:33.890
I asked my phone, I won't say which one,
where's the nearest gas station?

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:35.170
I've got a list of 16.

00:08:35.170 --> 00:08:39.690
I asked which ones were open, and it offered to search
the web for the phrase

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.080
which ones are open,

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:44.040
which would have not have
gotten me the answer I wanted.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:08:46.210
OK.

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:49.430
I think it's really important
for all of us building systems

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.750
to focus on the errors
the systems make,

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:55.390
rather than bragging about
the great successes that they have,

00:08:55.390 --> 00:08:57.330
because the errors are telling us

00:08:57.330 --> 00:09:00.620
where there's a mismatch
with our expectations.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:05.930
The same thing is true of Watson jeopardy,
and I hear that is now proving true

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:07.720
of deep learning systems

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:08.980
with captioning of images.

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.460
So look for the errors to understand
where there's a mismatch.

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:18.660
Finally there's an ethical argument
for considering working with people.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:24.640
Autonomous vehicles give a lot of promise,
the potential is huge.

00:09:24.640 --> 00:09:26.210
I'm very excited about them.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.720
But they have to be able to work
with us, otherwise they're going to run into trouble

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:34.580
and we've already
seen many of that.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:39.300
As wonderful as the cars are,
because we don't do what they expect.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.030
But hey, we're here first,
we're building them for us.

00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:44.360
People are a pain in the neck.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.230
How many people here have tried
to do experiments with people?

00:09:49.230 --> 00:09:51.690
Really, we don't behave very well.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:54.760
But too bad.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:55.900
You got to deal with them.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:00.440
And finally, there are all of these
really wonderful scientific challenges.

00:10:00.440 --> 00:10:03.430
So, let's go for it.

00:10:04.940 --> 00:10:06.040
I've listed some here.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.040
There are many, many more.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:13.850
The last one is actually
about the challenge of designing experiments

00:10:13.850 --> 00:10:16.140
that let us know
how people work.

00:10:17.630 --> 00:10:18.430
OK.

00:10:18.430 --> 00:10:22.400
So I want to shift now
from the argument for thinking about this,

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:24.100
to thinking about
the research.

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:29.320
There are many domains
in which this challenge arises,

00:10:29.320 --> 00:10:30.720
many kinds of teamwork.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:34.590
I'm going to mention those and then
I'm going to talk about some of my own work

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.080
in developing foundations.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:41.210
Theoretical foundations for modeling teamwork,
and then putting those to use.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:46.290
So education, health care,
writing papers, driving.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:53.200
We can think about groups of people
and groups of systems,

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.450
groups of people
that systems help.

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:58.190
The last two on this slide,
autonomous system,

00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:00.950
human collaborations
and crowd work present

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.800
really interesting challenges

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:06.010
where we as people can
teach machines to do better.

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:08.750
But we have to design them
so that they'll learn from us,

00:11:09.620 --> 00:11:11.590
and so that we're
teaching them things

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:12.860
that are interesting,

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:15.280
not making up,
not doing boring things

00:11:15.280 --> 00:11:17.280
because they are stupid.

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:18.960
OK.

00:11:18.960 --> 00:11:23.030
So shift to talking
about some work.

00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:29.860
There are several different theories
of collaboration that have been developed.

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.350
I'm going to talk about mine

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.510
because it has some features
I think are really important.

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:42.580
But all of them have mostly the
features I'm going to talk about.

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:47.890
So the first thing to note about
building systems that can be team members

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:50.790
is you have to design the team
work from the start,

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:52.630
and you'll see that
in the formalism

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:54.000
that I show you.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:12:03.720
And it's partly because teamwork is not
simply the sum of individual plans.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:08.750
You can't take my plan to do something
and Eric's plan to do something individually

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:10.120
and just smoosh them together.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:16.070
And you'll see that now in the theory
that I'm going to talk about.

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:18.690
So this is all in English.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.890
It was actually developed
as a formal theory in logic.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:27.390
I'm going to talk about this
as I give you these principles.

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:31.800
I want you to understand this is not just soft,
blah, blah, blah.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:35.710
But in fact, if any one of these principles
doesn't hold for a system,

00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:38.200
you can show it
will fail at teamwork

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.350
at some point in time.

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:47.150
So first we need each team member to
commit to the team doing its job.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:54.240
That commitment is framed
as an intention, which means that you have to believe

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.110
that the team
can actually do the work.

00:12:57.490 --> 00:13:00.860
You have to reach consensus on how
you're going to go about doing it,

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:04.700
and on who you're going
to allocate subtasks to.

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:08.420
Now that doesn't mean that everybody,
in fact it importantly

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:11.010
doesn't mean that everybody
has to know everything

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:12.360
about what everyone else is doing.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:18.450
But you need to know about the points of join,
so not all the details.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.350
Just with this much you can see
that there is a requirement

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:27.390
that the group have some decision
making processes.

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:31.970
They know when they've come to an agreement,
and that they have mutual belief

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:33.370
of the decisions that they have.

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:36.120
So we all have to know what we're all doing.

00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:41.340
In addition, two things that I'm going to illustrate
with some examples of research

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.430
are that we have to commit
to the tasks that we're given.

00:13:44.430 --> 00:13:50.190
And again, that commitment is an intention
and you can't commit to something you can't do.

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:51.650
That you know you can't do.

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:56.140
So our systems cannot commit
to doing things they can't do.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:01.620
You also have to commit to each other's success,
which means that you have to try to,

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:04.260
the systems have to be able to, when they act,

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:07.630
think about whether they are helping us
get our jobs done or interfering.

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:09.450
OK.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:12.270
So that's the delegation challenges.

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:17.730
We're going to talk about three different roles
for these theoretical frameworks.

00:14:18.310 --> 00:14:24.200
First, system specification, then design guide,
and then as an analytic framework.

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:29.580
And I hope that you'll take away from this
that not only are these roles,

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:33.190
but for each of these roles
there are different ways

00:14:33.190 --> 00:14:34.490
you could implement the system.

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:40.280
So you could use machine learning or Bayesian
reasoning or gridding theory or logical rules.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:46.800
It's the properties that the theory says the system
has to have that you need to get into the system

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:50.190
within whatever implementation
you're going to use.

00:14:51.620 --> 00:14:58.930
I want to read you a quote from Yao Shou
in a recent CACM article, when he was talking

00:14:58.930 --> 00:15:02.810
about the usefulness
of logically solid theories.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:09.080
He said, "This matters because it helps you build
software that's beautiful on the inside,

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.540
and if the internal structure isn't right,

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:16.200
if it doesn't cohere
with how humans think of the world,

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:19.510
you'll never have a truly
beautiful user experience."

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.210
So I hope you'll keep that in mind.

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:27.470
OK, so first theory as system specification.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:31.060
And I'm going to talk about the problem
of interruption management.

00:15:31.060 --> 00:15:37.220
So the shared plan principle here
is that you have to commit to each other's success.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:43.810
So if you're going to interrupt somebody,
you ought to have information that's valuable to them,

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:45.870
to interrupt only when it's useful.

00:15:47.190 --> 00:15:52.390
How many of you have been interrupted by dialog boxes
warning you of things like your battery dying

00:15:52.390 --> 00:15:54.640
in three hours
that you don't really care about now?

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:56.940
OK.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:02.050
So just go forward without me?

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:09.360
There we go.

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:14.480
So there are two insights that we had about
the interruption management problem,

00:16:15.340 --> 00:16:17.430
and these are insights from A.J.

00:16:17.430 --> 00:16:19.670
Comar's PhD dissertation work.

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:21.140
A.J.
is here now.

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:26.770
The first of these is that when we are
collaborating, most of the time,

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:28.600
most of what
we're doing is individual.

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:32.850
Remember, we break the tasks down into subtasks,
we delegate to other people.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:39.200
And if we take advantage of that
in your composability we can get computational savings.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:43.640
The second insight is that we have
to have efficient ways of reasoning

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:45.230
about each other's activities,

00:16:45.230 --> 00:16:49.210
even when we don't know in detail
what other people are doing.

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:52.190
So let me look at the first.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:16:57.180
This is work on seeing
that the problem can decompose

00:16:57.180 --> 00:16:58.650
into individual actions

00:16:58.650 --> 00:17:01.500
and joint actions, and then A.J.

00:17:01.500 --> 00:17:06.220
designed a formalism which he called
nearly decomposable MDPs.

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:11.960
The idea is you're maximizing
a joint function, but you have independent,

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:14.880
individual and joint
action sets,

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:17.680
and you decouple the transitions

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.640
depending on whether
there's a dependency,

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:21.200
when you need to communicate,

00:17:21.200 --> 00:17:23.070
or things are independent.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:29.570
The result of this is that you get
exponential efficiencies in computation time.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:32.020
You can actually compute things
you couldn't compute,

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:33.880
if you try to do it fully,

00:17:33.880 --> 00:17:36.140
as a fully coordinated system.

00:17:37.890 --> 00:17:38.520
OK.

00:17:38.520 --> 00:17:41.140
Second, the second problem... oops!

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:43.580
This is running ahead of me.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:48.540
OK.

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:51.540
I have this fear
it's on automatic advance.

00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:52.670
OK.

00:17:52.670 --> 00:17:58.230
So that's an example where there's
a specification that you commit to each other's success

00:18:02.690 --> 00:18:04.050
and therefore

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:06.870
that you don't interrupt
when you don't have something useful.

00:18:06.870 --> 00:18:12.000
The second example I want to use is work
on what we've called collaborative interfaces,

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:14.490
and this is in
an educational setting.

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:18.400
Here the domain challenge
is we have lots of interesting,

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:21.440
exploratory software for kids

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:24.750
in schools to learn
math and science

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:28.180
and evidence shows that
by exploring on their own,

00:18:28.180 --> 00:18:30.920
they learn more than
just being lectured at.

00:18:32.130 --> 00:18:36.270
The constraint that we get
from shared plans in designing these interfaces,

00:18:36.270 --> 00:18:39.790
is that you can't assign something
to the computer system

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:41.660
that it isn't
capable of doing.

00:18:41.660 --> 00:18:46.600
So the task allocation, so we looked at,
could we build interfaces that would help teachers

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:51.010
where we leave with the teachers
the things that they do best;

00:18:51.010 --> 00:18:53.060
so having broad
knowledge of students

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:54.290
and how to interact with them.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:19:00.220
But assign to the computer recognizing patterns
and what the students are doing.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:02.880
And I'll give you
two examples of this.

00:19:03.730 --> 00:19:09.610
On the left you see
an exploratory system for teaching middle school

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.430
students probability
and statistics.

00:19:12.430 --> 00:19:15.340
So what happens
when you flip a coin?

00:19:15.340 --> 00:19:20.870
And on the right you see a system
for teaching freshmen chemistry.

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:28.220
What we did in this work was to expand
on plan recognition algorithms,

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:31.610
the plan recognition algorithms
that had been developed in AI

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:34.260
up to this point
were all algorithms

00:19:34.260 --> 00:19:36.590
that assumed that
we were well-behaved.

00:19:37.430 --> 00:19:43.430
Eighth grade children,
given exploratory software,

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:46.180
not only make
mistakes sometimes,

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:47.960
that's part of it,

00:19:47.960 --> 00:19:50.750
so you have to look for things
that are not right and don't fit.

00:19:50.750 --> 00:19:53.560
But they also like playing
around and exploring in ways

00:19:53.560 --> 00:19:55.790
that maybe the designers of the system

00:19:55.790 --> 00:19:58.470
or the designers
of the problems hadn't thought.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:03.100
So the plan recognition algorithm had to deal
with those kinds of errant behaviors.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:06.350
And the result was that we developed
new plan recognition algorithms.

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:11.170
So this is a place where taking
on the challenge of building

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:12.920
something that worked well with people,

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:16.720
we actually came up
with hard scientific problems.

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:23.160
These systems were both validated off line
by through surveys of teachers

00:20:23.160 --> 00:20:26.660
and finding out
how well they worked,

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:29.740
and also proving
correctness of algorithms.

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:36.880
One of the most interesting responses
for me happened after the summit a few years ago.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:40.680
There was a workshop on AI
and education,

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:44.070
and the chemistry
faculty member at CMU

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.590
who had developed virtual labs

00:20:45.590 --> 00:20:48.240
said the interesting thing for him

00:20:48.240 --> 00:20:50.470
was that he learned from the system

00:20:50.470 --> 00:20:52.870
how his students
were solving problems

00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:55.280
and it wasn't the way
he thought they were.

00:20:55.280 --> 00:21:01.000
So this is part of what a system
can do to really help in education.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:04.730
But notice, we're not replacing the teacher;
we're complementing the teacher.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:08.980
More recently, Coby Gaule,
who's at Ben-Gurion University

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:11.720
who worked on S-Cast

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:13.470
and then did virtual labs,

00:21:13.470 --> 00:21:14.870
has been working

00:21:14.870 --> 00:21:17.970
So this was plan recognition
in a very different domain,

00:21:17.970 --> 00:21:20.010
teaching seventh grade geometry

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:22.910
where the students
are working in small groups,

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:24.510
and they're chatting
with each other.

00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:29.510
Again, the teacher can't listen
in on all the conversations,

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:32.490
and the technical question was,

00:21:32.490 --> 00:21:36.080
could you detect
critical moments

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.050
in those student conversations

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:40.060
and alert the teacher to groups

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.400
that needed to be encouraged
to do something else?

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:49.720
You can't do full
natural language processing, it's not just dialogue,

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:52.370
it's multiple students
talking at once at once.

00:21:52.370 --> 00:21:57.080
But they could use machine learning
to detect those critical moments.

00:21:57.080 --> 00:22:01.590
And they've just done some early
classroom validations

00:22:01.590 --> 00:22:04.960
that show that when you are using the software,

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:07.090
the teachers are actually intervening

00:22:07.090 --> 00:22:08.340
at better moments

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:10.820
and the duration
of interventions

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:12.860
is more appropriate
for what they are,

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:15.800
because they're not trying to do
too many things at once.

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:19.140
OK.

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.250
So the third thing
that I want to look at,

00:22:21.860 --> 00:22:25.300
my current research on
healthcare coordination.

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:30.810
And this is where we use shared plans
not for system design, at least not yet.

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:37.880
And not in a system specification way,
but to look at what was going wrong

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:39.370
in health care coordination.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:46.620
So the setting is clinics for children
with complex conditions.

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:51.720
This could be from genetic problems, it could be
from birth problems,

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:56.340
it could be from illness
at an early age.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:23:01.170
The children see somewhere
between 10 and 15 care providers.

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:05.990
No human being can keep
track of 14 other people.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.410
Some of us try,
but it's really not possible.

00:23:12.340 --> 00:23:18.060
So the domain challenge here is,
could we build a computer system

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:20.190
that would assist
the care providers

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.410
in keeping track of what
each other is doing?

00:23:23.000 --> 00:23:27.690
I'll just make a note that electronic
heath records does nothing to help with this problem.

00:23:27.690 --> 00:23:31.370
They may exacerbate it and I'll
give you an example in a minute.

00:23:33.790 --> 00:23:40.720
So this little sketch just shows you
how much communication goes on in different modalities

00:23:40.720 --> 00:23:42.750
among people on a typical team.

00:23:42.750 --> 00:23:45.570
You can feel very sorry
for the PCP in the middle,

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.190
who is like the quarterback and worse

00:23:48.190 --> 00:23:50.890
with no technology to help him.

00:23:51.880 --> 00:24:01.540
OK, what we learned
from looking at the work that people were doing was

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:04.020
that this was a very
different kind of teamwork

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:07.060
from the tightly coupled teamwork
we had studied before

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:09.970
and that our theories
had originally covered,

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:12.950
and that our plan recognition
algorithms were working in.

00:24:12.950 --> 00:24:15.740
So the first thing is the team
is relatively flat.

00:24:16.620 --> 00:24:20.220
The PCP might be in the middle of things,
but he's not in charge

00:24:20.220 --> 00:24:22.350
and he can't dictate
to other people what to do.

00:24:22.900 --> 00:24:29.340
They're also loosely coupled, which means
that they're like the nearly de-coupled plan.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:34.170
Sorry I'm not pushing the button.

00:24:34.170 --> 00:24:35.460
It's just working.

00:24:35.460 --> 00:24:38.010
It's autonomous.

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:39.710
I don't know how it became like that.

00:24:39.710 --> 00:24:42.420
It is not collaborating well with me.

00:24:42.420 --> 00:24:46.330
Like, I'm not there yet,
and I'm not running out of time yet.

00:24:46.330 --> 00:24:46.830
OK.

00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:51.500
So the bottom line here is,
we have a new kind of teamwork.

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:53.460
What we did before will not work.

00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:01.500
So the first thing we did, having observed
this by looking at what people,

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:04.310
the care providers
are doing, by talking to

00:25:05.600 --> 00:25:07.710
Oh wait, I forgot
this really great quote.

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:13.920
Sorry I have to go back to this, because it shows you
one of the things that for me

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.920
or the graduate student
working on this learned from,

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.370
asked the physicians
that she interviewed was,

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:21.990
was there ever a time
that they couldn't find the information

00:25:21.990 --> 00:25:25.440
they needed in the electronic health record.

00:25:25.440 --> 00:25:27.160
Here's my favorite example.

00:25:27.160 --> 00:25:30.620
Answer: there isn't an example
when I wasn't missing information.

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:36.990
And the parents say, we need to relay
information back and forth.

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:40.640
So the parents, who aren't medical experts,
are trying to figure out

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.500
what's important to tell people.

00:25:42.500 --> 00:25:45.730
We wanted to see if the system
could help with that.

00:25:46.320 --> 00:25:51.370
So here's what shared plans
would tell you about this setting.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:56.650
First, if they're going to
reach consensus on the recipe,

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:00.070
then we could provide
support for people

00:26:00.070 --> 00:26:02.450
not just making up a care plan
on a piece of paper

00:26:02.450 --> 00:26:05.870
that they put in a drawer
or the electronic equivalent,

00:26:05.870 --> 00:26:08.090
but something that's dynamic and active.

00:26:08.620 --> 00:26:12.750
That in fact takes into account
that as the child's condition changes,

00:26:12.750 --> 00:26:14.750
the plans evolve.

00:26:16.500 --> 00:26:21.200
We could support communication
and coordination at the appropriate levels.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.980
So figuring out who needs
to know what, when.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:32.920
In order to do this, we have to solve
a really hard information sharing challenge,

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:35.940
which is to figure out
who needs to know

00:26:35.940 --> 00:26:40.140
what when you don't have
the complete plan information.

00:26:40.140 --> 00:26:44.590
So not only don't you, it's not only
that you don't know

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:46.430
exactly what
somebody else is doing,

00:26:46.430 --> 00:26:50.640
so the PCP doesn't know exactly
what the GI doctor is doing,

00:26:50.640 --> 00:26:52.600
nor does the physical therapist,

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:55.750
but you may not even know
the full set of plans.

00:26:55.750 --> 00:27:00.740
You may not even have the probabilistic
recipe tree with a probability over them.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:04.790
OK.

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:12.660
So what Ofra did was to look
at this loosely coupled nature

00:27:12.660 --> 00:27:14.860
and recognize
that it was complemented

00:27:14.860 --> 00:27:16.950
by the extended duration

00:27:16.950 --> 00:27:18.690
of the work that people were doing.

00:27:18.690 --> 00:27:24.120
So it's not one-shot deals,
and you can take advantage of that

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:26.980
to learn collaboration
patterns over time.

00:27:26.980 --> 00:27:32.840
And from those collaboration patterns,
you can learn about task dependencies

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:34.600
and task allocation,

00:27:34.600 --> 00:27:36.360
and you can reason
about information

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:39.320
sharing based on those learned
collaboration patterns.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:45.750
Well, this need is not only in healthcare,
but also in various kinds of office work.

00:27:45.750 --> 00:27:49.440
For example, when we
write papers with each other, how many of you

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:50.770
when you're writing
papers with three

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:54.570
or four other people are sending e-mail
and making phone calls

00:27:54.570 --> 00:27:56.010
and getting together?
Right?

00:27:56.010 --> 00:27:59.110
As wonderful as track changes
is in Google Docs.

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:06.260
So what she did was
to implement the mutual influence

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:10.450
potential representation in the MIP DOI algorithm

00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:12.640
for collaborative writing;

00:28:13.500 --> 00:28:15.610
compared giving all changes,

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:17.460
which is what systems today do,

00:28:18.020 --> 00:28:20.250
with randomly filtering out some

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.920
so you have a smaller load,

00:28:21.920 --> 00:28:25.700
with personalized filtering
based on the MIP DOI algorithm.

00:28:27.160 --> 00:28:30.550
The results, very briefly,
were that not surprisingly,

00:28:30.550 --> 00:28:33.920
if you limit the number of edits

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.590
you show people
they're more productive,

00:28:35.590 --> 00:28:37.250
because they're looking
at fewer things.

00:28:39.190 --> 00:28:42.660
If you base the change, so this is
what's important about the algorithm.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:48.770
If you base the change on what
you've detected about interactions

00:28:48.770 --> 00:28:51.290
between the work
that people are doing,

00:28:52.440 --> 00:28:55.020
then it's judged more helpful
by the writers

00:28:55.020 --> 00:28:57.820
and independently the results

00:28:57.820 --> 00:29:00.060
are judged higher
quality by people

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:01.450
looking at what comes out.

00:29:03.320 --> 00:29:06.540
So this personalized change
awareness makes a difference.

00:29:06.540 --> 00:29:11.300
I'll just note here that this work
was really a wonderful blend of AI

00:29:11.300 --> 00:29:14.650
and HCI, and I think we're at a time

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.420
in the development of both fields

00:29:16.420 --> 00:29:18.710
where it's crucial for them
to work together.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:22.710
They split apart a long time ago,
and it's really important to come together.

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:29.580
So now I want to take a step back
and actually look at

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:31.520
some of the lessons
from the research

00:29:31.520 --> 00:29:34.530
that I've done over many decades.

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:41.660
And as a way of just summarizing
where we are with respect to this collaborative work

00:29:41.660 --> 00:29:45.800
and also leading into some
discussion of dialogues,

00:29:45.800 --> 00:29:48.170
because there's so much
going on in the dialogue

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:50.800
and language world these days.

00:29:51.960 --> 00:29:56.330
So the first thing, so I actually got
into collaboration by doing research on dialogue.

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:03.320
The first thing was that we noticed
that dialogues aren't linear sequences,

00:30:03.320 --> 00:30:05.430
they aren't random collections,

00:30:05.430 --> 00:30:07.080
they're actually structured,

00:30:07.080 --> 00:30:08.780
and you'll see an example
in a minute.

00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:14.660
And the language that we speak
indicates the boundaries

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:17.350
and indicates
discourse information.

00:30:18.480 --> 00:30:23.740
At the root of this structuring
of language is the intentions,

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:27.530
the purposes of the people
having the conversation.

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:33.100
And this is maybe the most important lesson
for those of you who are working on dialogue systems.

00:30:33.100 --> 00:30:36.620
It's not just
the streams of words.

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:41.780
It is the underlying purposes you need to understand
to really participate in a dialogue.

00:30:43.300 --> 00:30:43.990
OK.

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:47.340
We then moved in trying to,
Candy Sidner and I,

00:30:47.340 --> 00:30:49.540
in trying to move
from understanding

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:51.080
this to understanding

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:53.260
how to model
those intentions.

00:30:53.260 --> 00:30:56.170
And that's when I became
interested in collaboration,

00:30:57.530 --> 00:31:02.210
because you can't represent
intentional structure

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:04.080
as plans of individual agents.

00:31:05.280 --> 00:31:08.230
You have to, and that's where
shared plans came from.

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:13.580
And then there was a lot of,
we did it for dialogue

00:31:13.580 --> 00:31:16.390
and then a mathematician friend
of mine said to me,

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:19.220
come on, two, do N.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:24.080
And it turns out as any basketball coach
will tell you, N is a lot harder than two.

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:29.830
I think there's a big challenge here
at the edge already with this work,

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:32.430
which is how to integrate
the principles

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:33.660
from shared plans

00:31:33.660 --> 00:31:36.130
for dialogue and collaboration

00:31:36.130 --> 00:31:38.030
in a way that melds

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:40.010
with the data driven
ML approaches.

00:31:40.620 --> 00:31:43.090
How are we going to bring
these two together?

00:31:45.090 --> 00:31:49.280
Finally, from the collaboration
to loosely coupled plans,

00:31:49.280 --> 00:31:51.880
I told you about
that research.

00:31:54.130 --> 00:31:58.780
There is here also a challenge,
which is how do we do a broader challenge?

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:01.460
How do we deal with all
the different kinds of teamwork

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:02.920
and coordination

00:32:02.920 --> 00:32:04.250
which have widely

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:08.480
different information-sharing circumstances?

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:14.120
And now, before I move
on to the next thing I want to talk about,

00:32:14.120 --> 00:32:17.700
which is the ethics
aspect of all of this,

00:32:17.700 --> 00:32:21.450
I want to read a quote
from Zak Kohane who is very,

00:32:21.450 --> 00:32:25.440
very active in health informatics

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:30.700
and a big advocate
of using AI to understand

00:32:31.430 --> 00:32:34.560
and support diagnosis.

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:41.560
He has a mother who has a cardiac issue,
and he was interviewed about this.

00:32:43.060 --> 00:32:46.090
He said that on the one hand
it's really great to collect data

00:32:46.090 --> 00:32:47.330
and use AI for that.

00:32:48.090 --> 00:32:50.240
On the other hand,
there are family and friends.

00:32:50.240 --> 00:32:51.730
And here's the quote.

00:32:51.730 --> 00:32:55.410
"AI may be good at chess and go,
and developing expertise

00:32:55.410 --> 00:32:58.060
once reserved for doctors
in arcane areas

00:32:58.060 --> 00:32:59.660
such as reading X-rays."

00:32:59.660 --> 00:33:03.000
Radiologists might disagree
about it being arcane,

00:33:03.000 --> 00:33:04.480
but anyway.

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:08.240
"But AI does not do well at understanding
the wide world,

00:33:08.240 --> 00:33:10.760
at picking up mood
or subtle signs of distress,

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:14.400
at convincing a resistant human
to listen to the doctor.

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:15.830
We don't need AI for that.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:17.170
We need a caring village."

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:21.110
So again, an argument

00:33:21.110 --> 00:33:24.650
for the complementarity of AI,

00:33:24.650 --> 00:33:26.990
building systems
that complement us

00:33:27.570 --> 00:33:29.540
rather than thinking about systems

00:33:29.540 --> 00:33:32.740
that replace us
or simply replicate us.

00:33:33.310 --> 00:33:38.760
So now I want to turn to look
at the question of ethics.

00:33:39.460 --> 00:33:45.830
And I'm going to do that via an example
of a child's toy and dialogue.

00:33:47.240 --> 00:33:50.770
Because as I said in the beginning,
AI is out there in the world.

00:33:50.770 --> 00:33:56.170
We're using it all the time on phones
and for search and in many other ways,

00:33:56.890 --> 00:33:59.670
and it's even now
entering toys for children.

00:33:59.670 --> 00:34:05.840
And I think that really highlights
the importance of anyone

00:34:05.840 --> 00:34:08.100
building a system
with any AI in it.

00:34:08.100 --> 00:34:11.410
Frankly I think anyone
building a computer system that people are going to use

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:16.730
for anything other than programming need
to take into account.

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:23.340
So this is for those of you who didn't see
this article in the fall of 2015.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:26.330
Mattel announced a new Barbie
doll that could talk,

00:34:26.330 --> 00:34:29.590
and Mattel advertised

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:31.890
it as your daughter's
new best friend.

00:34:31.890 --> 00:34:35.580
Here's an example dialogue
taken from the New York Times.

00:34:35.580 --> 00:34:40.090
I teach a course on intelligent systems
design and ethical challenges.

00:34:40.090 --> 00:34:43.060
And the students in the course
bought a Barbie doll,

00:34:43.560 --> 00:34:47.180
and found the 300 pages
of scripts

00:34:47.180 --> 00:34:49.040
that the Barbie doll follows.

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:51.830
So there are many more examples
that are even worse than this,

00:34:51.830 --> 00:34:53.800
but we'll deal with this.

00:34:53.800 --> 00:35:00.730
So Barbie knows this kid has a sister,
and asks, what nice things does she do for you?

00:35:01.660 --> 00:35:03.470
The child says nothing.

00:35:03.470 --> 00:35:05.700
Barbie says, what's
the last nice thing?

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:09.350
OK, Barbie hasn't
understood the word nothing.

00:35:10.940 --> 00:35:12.580
Not in the script.

00:35:12.580 --> 00:35:16.730
It's this little word, negatives are hard,
anyone who studies natural language knows that.

00:35:17.240 --> 00:35:18.080
OK.

00:35:18.080 --> 00:35:23.090
The child tries to answer,
but it's got another kind of negative word.

00:35:23.090 --> 00:35:24.520
Destroyed it.

00:35:24.520 --> 00:35:25.770
Barbie says, oh yeah.

00:35:25.770 --> 00:35:27.770
Tell me more.

00:35:29.430 --> 00:35:31.730
The kid gets a little annoyed.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:33.730
I left that part out.

00:35:35.980 --> 00:35:37.060
More than a little annoyed.

00:35:37.060 --> 00:35:41.240
And Barbie says, have you told
your sister lately how cool she is?

00:35:42.700 --> 00:35:45.880
At this point, I believe
the child said, that's it Barbie.

00:35:51.050 --> 00:35:53.270
I forgot that part.
OK.

00:35:53.270 --> 00:35:54.520
So here's the thing.

00:35:54.520 --> 00:35:59.260
This is not just awkward but it raises,
I mean this is kind of funny for us,

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:00.920
but the whole idea

00:36:00.920 --> 00:36:03.320
of having these kinds
of interchanges

00:36:03.320 --> 00:36:06.600
with a kid raises
some ethical concerns.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:09.970
And I'm just going to talk
about two of them, there were many more.

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:16.140
One is expectations that are violated
with respect to dialogue behavior,

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:18.090
and the fact that this doll

00:36:18.090 --> 00:36:22.850
as my philosophy student
PhD student

00:36:25.300 --> 00:36:27.780
teaching fellow said,
unremittingly pleasant,

00:36:27.780 --> 00:36:29.710
like no human being
I've ever met.

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:34.640
Kind of this emotional
equivalent of being too skinny.

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:40.130
OK.
So language learning.

00:36:40.130 --> 00:36:42.760
The doll is designed for three
to eight-year-old children.

00:36:42.760 --> 00:36:47.070
They're supposed to be learning language
and how we have carry-on dialogues.

00:36:49.210 --> 00:36:52.600
What they're learning is you
just ignore what the other person says,

00:36:52.600 --> 00:36:54.440
and go along with
what you're saying.

00:36:54.440 --> 00:37:00.580
This is not exactly what we would like to
teach three to eight-year-old children, boys or girls.

00:37:02.580 --> 00:37:04.590
How about child development and friendship?

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:07.260
So unremittingly pleasant.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:09.390
How many of you are unremittingly pleasant?

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:12.810
I didn't think so.

00:37:12.810 --> 00:37:14.090
Thank you for being honest.

00:37:16.000 --> 00:37:19.530
You know, we don't want a three-year-old
to think she has to be unremittingly pleasant.

00:37:21.800 --> 00:37:27.840
On the other hand, we do want a child
to know that there's a penalty to pay

00:37:27.840 --> 00:37:29.580
if you're mean to somebody,

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:31.750
and Barbie doesn't care
what the child says.

00:37:31.750 --> 00:37:33.940
It just goes along with its script.

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:37.610
I'll just make a note here that I have
some worries about anybody

00:37:37.610 --> 00:37:40.700
who thinks we should create
the Westworld environment,

00:37:41.700 --> 00:37:44.990
given that most of us
would stop somebody

00:37:44.990 --> 00:37:47.100
beating a dog on the street.

00:37:47.100 --> 00:37:50.780
Why do we want to teach people that it's OK
to do what goes on in Westworld?

00:37:50.780 --> 00:37:52.720
I don't know.
Anyway.

00:37:52.720 --> 00:37:54.310
All right, so there are other questions.

00:37:54.900 --> 00:38:00.330
Questions about trust and creativity,
but I won't be able to go into those.

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:00.660
OK.

00:38:00.660 --> 00:38:02.220
You can ask me about them later.

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:04.210
OK so it's not just Barbie.

00:38:04.920 --> 00:38:10.190
It's any approach to dialogue
that misses some of the key facts

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:12.030
we know about human dialogue.

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:16.900
And one of those is that dialogues
are structured by purpose.

00:38:17.820 --> 00:38:19.750
They're not just linear sequences.

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:24.190
So here's one of my favorite examples
of why that matters.

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:29.560
Most of us were taught
that a pronoun referred to the last thing

00:38:29.560 --> 00:38:31.910
that it matched in number and gender.

00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:38.060
The last thing that them in blue matches
is number and gender is kids.

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:42.100
How many of you think that what
this person means is that they put the kids away?

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:45.410
How many of you, I just have to see
if you can raise your hands.

00:38:45.410 --> 00:38:47.230
How many of you think
it's something else?

00:38:48.220 --> 00:38:49.470
OK.

00:38:49.470 --> 00:38:52.170
So here's what actually
went on in this dialogue.

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:54.120
There was some intonation.

00:38:54.850 --> 00:39:00.960
John came by and left the groceries. Stop
that you kids. And I put them away after he left.

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:04.510
There's no question you didn't have
a question about what's going on.

00:39:05.050 --> 00:39:07.580
Now this is a narrative
about groceries.

00:39:07.580 --> 00:39:11.860
I have to say I was in the airport
on Saturday, my plane looked like it would be canceled.

00:39:11.860 --> 00:39:13.960
I called the MSR travel people.

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:19.230
I could have had exactly the same conversation
but with it being about travel interrupted,

00:39:19.230 --> 00:39:21.300
by saying something
to the noisy kids

00:39:21.300 --> 00:39:23.210
who were unhappy their plane
might be canceled.

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:25.780
OK.

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:30.280
So finally, dialogue
is not just adjacency pairs.

00:39:30.830 --> 00:39:34.410
It's Twitter conversations aside,
but you know, they have their limitations.

00:39:35.570 --> 00:39:37.670
It's not just
question-response pairs.

00:39:38.190 --> 00:39:43.350
So just from looking at this, you can see some of
the challenges for chat bots.

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:49.470
Open domains are going to be harder
than closed domains because you have to deal with many

00:39:49.470 --> 00:39:50.770
more kinds of purposes.

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:57.480
Short dialogues that look just
like question-answer pairs are much easier

00:39:57.480 --> 00:39:59.170
than when you get
into a real dialogue.

00:40:00.520 --> 00:40:00.800
OK.

00:40:00.800 --> 00:40:03.480
What are the roots
of the ethical challenges here?

00:40:04.210 --> 00:40:07.340
Well we already saw
missing dialogue capabilities.

00:40:08.760 --> 00:40:11.710
It's also the case that if you
give people something,

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.090
if they think a system can do one thing,

00:40:15.090 --> 00:40:18.060
they will generalize to its
being able to do another.

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:21.630
So the last time I asked my phone
where the nearest E.R.

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:27.110
was, I did this, I gave a bunch of talks for phi
beta kappa traveling around the country.

00:40:27.110 --> 00:40:29.120
It always could told me
where the nearest E.R.

00:40:29.120 --> 00:40:30.760
was.

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:34.490
When I asked where I can get a flu shot,
it understood what I said,

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:37.300
but this is answer gave me.

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:39.970
Actually two different answers.

00:40:39.970 --> 00:40:41.530
I don't know why I can't figure this out.

00:40:43.040 --> 00:40:46.920
More importantly, when I asked
where can I go to get a sprained ankle treated,

00:40:47.710 --> 00:40:49.710
this is the answer I got.

00:40:50.420 --> 00:40:55.620
Now it's not so bad, really, to tell me
how to treat the sprained ankle.

00:40:56.120 --> 00:40:58.820
But it would have been really bad
if this had been about a heart attack.

00:41:01.710 --> 00:41:03.710
OK.

00:41:06.580 --> 00:41:08.450
So we need to get it right.

00:41:09.290 --> 00:41:12.850
We need to be clear about what
the competence is and what it's not.

00:41:12.850 --> 00:41:17.970
And we need to take seriously this idea
that we can't build systems that commit

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:20.250
to doing something
they're not capable of doing.

00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:24.970
But there are many other
ethical challenges that come up,

00:41:24.970 --> 00:41:27.410
and they affect us
not just as individuals,

00:41:27.410 --> 00:41:29.390
but they can affect
whole communities,

00:41:29.390 --> 00:41:32.030
they can affect
the world at large.

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:36.600
And I have just some of them here
that I hope people will think about.

00:41:37.300 --> 00:41:41.720
Privacy and security
is always brought up, but there's also a place

00:41:41.720 --> 00:41:43.880
where there's a clash of the incentives

00:41:43.880 --> 00:41:45.720
for business and social good.

00:41:45.720 --> 00:41:52.450
And I'm really glad to see things
like the ETHER effort here at Microsoft

00:41:52.450 --> 00:41:54.230
and the Partnership for AI,

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:57.380
because it's really crucial
that companies think about this.

00:41:57.380 --> 00:42:03.290
So we saw this clash in the concern
for getting more clicks

00:42:03.290 --> 00:42:06.250
and selling more ads
in the spread of fake news.

00:42:07.500 --> 00:42:12.030
There are also questions of justice,
and Eric brought up some of this

00:42:12.030 --> 00:42:13.490
in his talk about bias.

00:42:14.320 --> 00:42:18.230
If you're predicting the future
based on the past, at the same time

00:42:18.230 --> 00:42:20.160
that you're trying
to change communities

00:42:20.160 --> 00:42:22.150
and lower crime,
for example,

00:42:22.150 --> 00:42:26.150
then there's a clash between
this progressive cultural change

00:42:26.150 --> 00:42:28.130
you want and the predictions
that you're making.

00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:35.570
Also, and this came up in a
State Department workshop I chaired last fall.

00:42:35.570 --> 00:42:38.320
There's great concern
about who owns the data.

00:42:39.100 --> 00:42:41.830
And here we have data, it is not
just Google and Facebook,

00:42:41.830 --> 00:42:45.090
by the way, it's the cars
that people are driving.

00:42:45.090 --> 00:42:47.050
Who owns that data?

00:42:47.050 --> 00:42:48.330
How is it going to be shared?

00:42:49.120 --> 00:42:51.720
Who's going to determine
what regulations there are?

00:42:53.070 --> 00:42:57.920
And then there are questions of equality,
and making sure that the benefits of the AI systems

00:42:57.920 --> 00:42:59.920
we develop

00:43:01.300 --> 00:43:04.100
are spread around the world
and of building trust.

00:43:04.100 --> 00:43:05.930
Well, there's a much longer list.

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:14.530
The second thing I want to say is that
when you think about AI and ethics,

00:43:14.530 --> 00:43:16.980
we should think not only
about not doing harm,

00:43:16.980 --> 00:43:19.020
which is really important,
but about doing good.

00:43:19.580 --> 00:43:25.350
And there was recently a AAAI
spring symposium in which people talked about many,

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:28.100
many different areas
including health care,

00:43:28.100 --> 00:43:29.950
including sustainability.

00:43:29.950 --> 00:43:34.140
I was glad to learn that
MSR has an effort on AI for Earth.

00:43:34.140 --> 00:43:40.050
And so I think we really could all be thinking about,
there's just many, many aspects of life

00:43:40.050 --> 00:43:41.670
where we could be building systems

00:43:41.670 --> 00:43:44.150
that would be doing good,

00:43:44.150 --> 00:43:46.350
and not just worry about
what we shouldn't be building.

00:43:48.590 --> 00:43:52.090
OK, so then one of the issues
that people think about a lot,

00:43:52.970 --> 00:43:54.670
and I think we as AI people

00:43:54.670 --> 00:43:57.480
should think about
as computer scientists, by the way.

00:43:57.480 --> 00:43:59.330
I don't think this is just an AI problem.

00:43:59.330 --> 00:44:02.410
It's a problem of all the technology
that we're building.

00:44:03.920 --> 00:44:08.180
It is about jobs, and about whether
we're replacing people,

00:44:08.180 --> 00:44:10.720
augmenting people or complementing people.

00:44:10.720 --> 00:44:15.670
So let me just give an example
from the legal and policy arena,

00:44:15.670 --> 00:44:17.160
where we can think about things

00:44:17.160 --> 00:44:19.370
that already computers
are really great at,

00:44:21.780 --> 00:44:24.250
like document retrieval and search.

00:44:24.250 --> 00:44:29.310
Things that they can do adequately
and well enough in some areas

00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:32.430
like drafting straightforward documents.

00:44:32.430 --> 00:44:36.930
And things that at least, in the near-term future,
they're unlikely to do well,

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:41.460
like producing a new persuasive
foreign policy initiative.

00:44:43.580 --> 00:44:48.210
So that's the law environment,
the policy environment.

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:52.160
Health care is similarly nuanced,
as is education.

00:44:54.860 --> 00:44:57.450
So the idea that we're going
to replace doctors.

00:44:57.450 --> 00:45:00.630
Rather than thinking about how we can
replace them to save money,

00:45:00.630 --> 00:45:03.340
we could think about how to build systems

00:45:03.340 --> 00:45:07.300
that will enable them
to do their jobs more effectively,

00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:10.620
just as we were doing with the teachers
in the examples I gave you.

00:45:11.890 --> 00:45:12.350
OK.

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:14.440
So these are all high level things.

00:45:14.440 --> 00:45:19.900
I think there's some things that as AI people,
as technology people, as computer scientists,

00:45:19.900 --> 00:45:21.810
we should be thinking
about right now

00:45:21.810 --> 00:45:24.830
because these are things
we can directly control.

00:45:24.830 --> 00:45:26.770
We can't control foreign policy.

00:45:27.490 --> 00:45:31.850
And that's the question of the jobs now,
not just in the future.

00:45:32.430 --> 00:45:36.910
And here I've just given you two examples,
one is crowd-sourced piecework.

00:45:38.260 --> 00:45:42.860
OK, so people working,
under what conditions?

00:45:42.860 --> 00:45:45.130
Who regulates those conditions and how?

00:45:47.300 --> 00:45:54.950
The second thing is as we think about designing systems
that have some AI capabilities, we could think about,

00:45:54.950 --> 00:45:57.990
and we would think about them
as complementing people

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:00.110
rather than mostly replacing people.

00:46:00.110 --> 00:46:06.300
And when they can't do it they just fail,
and then some poor person has to take over

00:46:06.980 --> 00:46:08.610
and make up for their failures,

00:46:08.610 --> 00:46:10.520
which happens to me all the time

00:46:10.520 --> 00:46:13.020
when I get an automated
customer care agent.

00:46:13.920 --> 00:46:18.710
We might think about figuring out a way
to build a system that would help,

00:46:18.710 --> 00:46:22.020
let's say those customer care agents
do their jobs better.

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:32.240
So I would like people to think about that,
because I think we can do better

00:46:32.240 --> 00:46:34.200
than we have done so far.

00:46:34.200 --> 00:46:39.480
So far we have thought about, wow, could we replace
the person, OK if it fails it goes to the person.

00:46:39.480 --> 00:46:47.600
That makes the person's job one of handling,
trying to figure out what the other person,

00:46:47.600 --> 00:46:49.640
really the person who called in really wanted,

00:46:49.640 --> 00:46:53.000
and how to make them happy now
that they're extremely unhappy.

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:57.000
Rather than enabling a person
to establish a relationship with somebody,

00:46:57.000 --> 00:46:59.270
and then get them
an answer more quickly.

00:47:00.330 --> 00:47:04.420
So let me turn to some general
ethical principles.

00:47:06.670 --> 00:47:12.320
People have asked about who should decide the values,
and I think that's a really important thing

00:47:12.320 --> 00:47:14.080
to leave with people, not machines.

00:47:15.290 --> 00:47:20.520
I also want to urge people to stop thinking
about and help those of us

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:22.670
who are already working
with the press and others,

00:47:22.670 --> 00:47:26.410
to get them to stop thinking
about robots taking over,

00:47:26.410 --> 00:47:29.580
because it's not happening anytime soon

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:31.970
and it's a distraction from issues

00:47:31.970 --> 00:47:33.700
that are important now.

00:47:34.420 --> 00:47:40.540
So I personally think we have more to fear
from dumb systems that somebody has told us are smart,

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:44.220
than from intelligent systems
that know their limits.

00:47:44.220 --> 00:47:48.300
And I know that Eric talked earlier
about unknowns and known unknowns.

00:47:48.300 --> 00:47:52.160
I think this is really a system,
a really important thing to aim

00:47:52.160 --> 00:47:54.540
for is a system that knows its limits.

00:47:56.370 --> 00:48:01.920
So there are different ways for handling
the ethical challenges that come up.

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:06.180
The one we hear about most
is regulation and policy.

00:48:07.790 --> 00:48:09.400
There's also design.

00:48:09.400 --> 00:48:13.690
We can design the systems differently,
and that's really the main message

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:15.350
I want people to take away from here,

00:48:15.350 --> 00:48:17.530
is that we can design systems differently.

00:48:20.040 --> 00:48:22.990
One of the things that many people
are thinking about is,

00:48:22.990 --> 00:48:26.740
could we build in ethical reasoning capabilities?

00:48:26.740 --> 00:48:30.100
And that would be great, but it's hard enough
to build in reasoning capabilities,

00:48:30.100 --> 00:48:32.950
so I think this is a long term aspiration.

00:48:32.950 --> 00:48:38.880
Certainly an interesting research area,
but I'd like to see us focus on design right now.

00:48:39.830 --> 00:48:44.190
And finally, when we put systems
out in the world,

00:48:44.190 --> 00:48:47.340
we could accurately describe
their capabilities.

00:48:47.340 --> 00:48:51.570
Mattel should not have said the Barbie doll
was going to be your daughter's next best friend.

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:57.210
OK.

00:48:57.210 --> 00:49:01.810
So I'm actually unclear whether this is
my time or the total time.

00:49:03.460 --> 00:49:04.530
OK.
- That's the total time.

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:06.120
But we'll make it work.

00:49:06.120 --> 00:49:07.800
- OK.
All right.

00:49:07.800 --> 00:49:12.480
So let me, this is my last major point.

00:49:13.230 --> 00:49:17.450
I said earlier that teamwork capabilities had
to be designed in from the start.

00:49:18.170 --> 00:49:21.700
Ethics also has to be
designed in from the start.

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:24.650
And to get it right in the future,
we need to do two things.

00:49:25.270 --> 00:49:31.040
One is those of us who teach need
to integrate ethics into our curriculum.

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:34.120
We're doing this at Harvard now.

00:49:34.120 --> 00:49:39.390
We experimented this past spring with having
a small ethics module in four courses.

00:49:40.120 --> 00:49:43.820
We're going to do six in the fall.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:48.040
A quick example from my class.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:52.940
They study the Facebook emotion
contagion experiment on a Tuesday.

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:55.960
Lots of discussion of ethics,
by the way.

00:49:55.960 --> 00:49:59.920
These students, 150 students
applied for a class of 30,

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:02.810
so they really wanted to get in
and they cared about ethics.

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:09.120
Forty-eight hours later on Thursday,
I gave an assignment and I did an in-class activity

00:50:09.120 --> 00:50:10.900
on designing
a recommender system,

00:50:11.550 --> 00:50:15.010
including needing to list
the characteristics

00:50:15.010 --> 00:50:16.710
of the users they would collect.

00:50:17.700 --> 00:50:20.490
After they reported out,
I asked how many of them

00:50:20.490 --> 00:50:23.060
had thought about the ethical consequences

00:50:23.060 --> 00:50:25.720
of the information
they were collecting.

00:50:25.720 --> 00:50:27.570
None of them, none.

00:50:27.570 --> 00:50:29.160
Well actually, this year one.

00:50:29.160 --> 00:50:30.670
Last year, none.

00:50:30.670 --> 00:50:35.000
If we're focused on efficient,
effective design with all this stuff,

00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:36.540
you know efficient code, et cetera,

00:50:36.540 --> 00:50:39.040
et cetera and ethics
isn't on the list,

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:41.050
it ain't going to happen.

00:50:41.050 --> 00:50:42.400
So that's the second part.

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:47.380
Companies building systems need to make ethics
part of the product design.

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:48.860
OK.

00:50:48.860 --> 00:50:52.030
I will end by saying it's not smart
if it doesn't work with us,

00:50:52.030 --> 00:50:53.280
and we want it
pulling together,

00:50:53.280 --> 00:50:54.840
not pulling against us.

00:50:56.440 --> 00:50:58.940
I want systems that make us
feel smarter, not dumber.

00:50:59.780 --> 00:51:01.740
And conjectures are really important.

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:05.310
Anybody who is early stage
and wants to know

00:51:05.310 --> 00:51:06.570
how Chomsky
and McCarthy

00:51:06.570 --> 00:51:09.220
discouraged me,
can ask me later.

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:11.990
They told me what I was
doing was impossible or useless.

00:51:13.250 --> 00:51:16.760
And when we first did shared plans,
people told us to think harder

00:51:16.760 --> 00:51:18.440
and we could do it
as individual plans.

00:51:18.440 --> 00:51:21.860
So if you're young and you
have a good idea, go with it.

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:23.840
We need people to take risks now.

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:26.700
Computers aren't islands.

00:51:26.700 --> 00:51:28.260
We've got to do this.

00:51:28.260 --> 00:51:31.470
These are all the people
that helped me, including the funders.

00:51:31.470 --> 00:51:32.880
And I'm happy for questions.

00:51:33.790 --> 00:51:39.500
- Thank you, Barbara.

00:51:39.500 --> 00:51:41.000
We have time for a few questions.

00:51:41.000 --> 00:51:43.830
There are people running
around with a paddle,

00:51:43.830 --> 00:51:46.520
so if you have a question,
please raise your hand.

00:51:51.100 --> 00:51:53.340
And please say who you are and where you're from.

00:52:01.520 --> 00:52:02.050
- Hi.

00:52:02.050 --> 00:52:04.000
Brent, I'm from UC Berkeley.

00:52:04.000 --> 00:52:04.870
- Wait, wait.
Where are you?

00:52:04.870 --> 00:52:06.160
I didn't even call on anyone yet.

00:52:06.160 --> 00:52:07.510
- Over here.

00:52:07.510 --> 00:52:09.090
- OK.

00:52:09.090 --> 00:52:10.340
- Paddle 3.

00:52:10.340 --> 00:52:11.600
- Hi, I'm from UC Berkeley.

00:52:11.600 --> 00:52:13.010
Thanks for the wonderful talk.

00:52:13.010 --> 00:52:15.770
I had a question about foundations for teamwork.

00:52:15.770 --> 00:52:20.170
You talked about how designing it from the start
is really critical for success.

00:52:20.170 --> 00:52:26.330
How do these other, higher order,
social activities build into that foundation?

00:52:26.330 --> 00:52:28.820
What is sort of the status today?

00:52:28.820 --> 00:52:32.260
Things like competition, justice.

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:33.260
You mentioned ethics a little bit.

00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:34.460
How does that factor in?

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:39.440
- Well, that's for people here and their students
and post docs to start working on.

00:52:44.480 --> 00:52:48.380
Number one over there.
- Hi.

00:52:48.380 --> 00:52:49.580
My name is Richard Banks.

00:52:49.580 --> 00:52:52.200
I run the design team in Microsoft

00:52:52.200 --> 00:52:53.420
Cambridge Lab,

00:52:53.420 --> 00:52:56.620
and I spent a long time
with design students particularly

00:52:56.620 --> 00:52:58.150
in the interaction design discipline.

00:52:58.150 --> 00:53:03.490
And I'm wondering, what way do you think
we should be educating these students

00:53:03.490 --> 00:53:05.270
who will be forming
the design part

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:07.190
of a multi-disciplinary teams,

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:08.950
in terms of both understanding AI

00:53:08.950 --> 00:53:10.330
as a technology,

00:53:10.330 --> 00:53:13.580
and starting to deal
with some of the ethical issues

00:53:13.580 --> 00:53:15.580
that you were describing?

00:53:16.260 --> 00:53:17.450
- That's terrific.

00:53:17.450 --> 00:53:21.080
And I can tell you best by telling you
a bit about my course,

00:53:21.080 --> 00:53:24.820
which is that it's aimed at students
who have had one programming course.

00:53:24.820 --> 00:53:30.440
I co-teach it with somebody who is either
a philosophy graduate student or next,

00:53:30.440 --> 00:53:33.810
this coming year,
actually a philosophy faculty member.

00:53:33.810 --> 00:53:39.780
I think it's really, really important to have somebody
who has deep expertise in ethics there with you.

00:53:39.780 --> 00:53:43.680
Almost, and let me tell you about what
we did at Harvard in the spring.

00:53:43.680 --> 00:53:51.780
We had a recent PhD in ethics meet with faculty
to talk about the systems, the courses,

00:53:51.780 --> 00:53:53.400
and what they were teaching and then

00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:56.250
find a particular example
where ethics mattered.

00:53:56.250 --> 00:54:00.200
So for example, in the HCI class,
they looked at accessibility.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:05.070
Many apps are not accessible
to people who are blind or deaf,

00:54:05.070 --> 00:54:07.800
so that was an issue
that they talked about.

00:54:09.270 --> 00:54:12.500
Needless to say, in social
networking, they talked about fake news.

00:54:13.420 --> 00:54:17.430
So you can find, that's what
I would recommend.

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:20.780
There have got to be a lot of good
people who are philosophers

00:54:20.780 --> 00:54:23.130
and who have written,
who have studied ethics.

00:54:23.130 --> 00:54:25.880
In fact, the Trolley problem
comes out of Britain, by the way.

00:54:27.420 --> 00:54:31.900
And really, so make it
an integrated part of the design

00:54:32.580 --> 00:54:34.130
that they're doing and the projects

00:54:34.130 --> 00:54:36.330
that they're working on,
but bring it.

00:54:36.330 --> 00:54:39.640
So I'm a disbeliever in professional ethics.

00:54:39.640 --> 00:54:42.850
I think that's if you follow these rules,
you won't get sued.

00:54:42.850 --> 00:54:48.380
You really, really have to go deeply
to become a thinker about ethical issues.

00:54:49.570 --> 00:54:51.570
- OK, thanks.
Number two.

00:54:52.540 --> 00:54:55.250
- Hi, Gershon Dublon from MIT Media Lab.

00:54:56.150 --> 00:55:00.840
So one of the last things you said was we
need systems that make us feel smart.

00:55:00.840 --> 00:55:05.630
And I wondered if you could comment
on this question of kind of,

00:55:05.630 --> 00:55:07.980
how long it should take
to learn to use the system?

00:55:07.980 --> 00:55:09.680
How easy should they be?

00:55:09.680 --> 00:55:12.740
And I ask that because I mean,
teamwork as an example,

00:55:12.740 --> 00:55:14.660
is not something that is
necessarily something that

00:55:14.660 --> 00:55:17.410
we're well-suited for at the outset.

00:55:17.410 --> 00:55:19.960
We learn to work together
over long periods of time.

00:55:20.540 --> 00:55:25.500
And as we build these AI systems, what if it takes
two years to learn how to work with them?

00:55:26.080 --> 00:55:29.300
How should we kind of think about that question?

00:55:29.300 --> 00:55:29.810
- OK.

00:55:29.810 --> 00:55:34.150
So first on systems in general,
I'll just quote Don Norman,

00:55:34.150 --> 00:55:37.840
who said for those of you not in HCI,

00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:41.870
a really important person
in the HCI community.

00:55:42.700 --> 00:55:46.380
He said, I don't need a manual to use my toaster.

00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:47.830
Of course that might not be true anymore.

00:55:54.090 --> 00:56:00.460
So, it's true that as people we need
to learn with respect to each team

00:56:00.460 --> 00:56:02.330
how to work with those team members.

00:56:02.330 --> 00:56:08.210
I think it should take no longer to learn
how to work with a team computer agents

00:56:08.210 --> 00:56:10.060
than it takes
to learn with each other,

00:56:10.060 --> 00:56:13.070
and that depends on the people and whether they've,

00:56:13.070 --> 00:56:16.510
how much they've been parts
of teams before or not.

00:56:16.510 --> 00:56:22.410
But the systems, I think we should design
to be the best of team players.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:24.510
- OK, thanks.

00:56:24.510 --> 00:56:28.150
The last two questions
have been from Cambridge, and continuing that trend,

00:56:28.150 --> 00:56:30.600
I think question number three was
from Cambridge as well.

00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:32.800
No more Cambridge
questions after this.

00:56:32.800 --> 00:56:33.730
- Hi.

00:56:33.730 --> 00:56:36.950
Other Cambridge, David Karger, MIT.

00:56:36.950 --> 00:56:38.200
- Hi David.
- Hi.

00:56:38.200 --> 00:56:42.150
And I guess alumnus of your 1988 AI class.

00:56:42.990 --> 00:56:48.220
So you talked about the sort of foundational
principles of collaboration,

00:56:48.220 --> 00:56:49.810
your theory of collaboration and such.

00:56:49.810 --> 00:56:52.710
It struck me as interesting
because you talked about sort of the need

00:56:52.710 --> 00:56:54.310
for consensus around goals

00:56:55.440 --> 00:56:56.830
and shared plans
and things.

00:56:56.830 --> 00:56:58.400
One of your colleagues, Madu Sudan,

00:56:58.400 --> 00:57:00.130
did some really interesting work a few years ago

00:57:00.130 --> 00:57:01.880
on communication
with aliens,

00:57:02.520 --> 00:57:04.860
and sort of developed a theory about

00:57:04.860 --> 00:57:07.790
how you could actually
carry out communication

00:57:07.790 --> 00:57:09.930
and say the communication
was happening,

00:57:09.930 --> 00:57:14.740
even when the two parties
had no shared goals,

00:57:14.740 --> 00:57:17.960
no shared understanding
of the universe.

00:57:18.650 --> 00:57:26.390
And I'm wondering whether that sort of,
you know, how that relates to your arguments

00:57:26.390 --> 00:57:29.060
that you have to have
these consensus goals?

00:57:29.060 --> 00:57:32.300
And bringing it closer to home,
if you think about user studies,

00:57:32.800 --> 00:57:35.830
where subjects are often not aware
of what they're doing,

00:57:35.830 --> 00:57:39.500
or are even deceived
about what their goals are.

00:57:39.500 --> 00:57:41.550
How does that play into
what you were talking about?

00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:47.310
- So, remember what I said is
if the principles are violated,

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:48.950
you don't have real teamwork.

00:57:48.950 --> 00:57:54.670
So the answer with Madu is to do it justice,
is much longer,

00:57:54.670 --> 00:57:56.560
but he and I actually disagree on

00:57:56.560 --> 00:57:58.360
when you have communication and dialogue.

00:58:00.670 --> 00:58:06.850
So, and I would have to look at the user studies
to give you a particular answer,

00:58:06.850 --> 00:58:09.360
but it's not saying,
you can have things

00:58:09.360 --> 00:58:13.520
that look like teamwork
happen accidentally.

00:58:13.520 --> 00:58:17.890
But if you don't have these conditions,
I can take that group of people

00:58:17.890 --> 00:58:20.810
and whatever they're trying to do
and give you an example

00:58:20.810 --> 00:58:22.920
where it would fall apart
and they would fail.

00:58:22.920 --> 00:58:25.210
So that's the point of the principles.

00:58:25.210 --> 00:58:31.110
It's not that you can't. Like you can have a system
that looks like, remember my point about errors?

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:35.930
You can have a system that looks like it's smart
until it looks like it's really dumb.

00:58:36.580 --> 00:58:41.380
So you can have something that looks like
it's working like teamwork, and then it falls apart.

00:58:41.380 --> 00:58:44.720
I can give you an example from the 1990s at Harvard.

00:58:44.720 --> 00:58:46.960
I love this example.

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:48.740
Women Basketball.

00:58:49.470 --> 00:58:53.390
The Ivy League always sends
one team to the NCAAs.

00:58:53.390 --> 00:58:55.620
Harvard went.

00:58:55.620 --> 00:58:57.550
Their first match was with Stanford,

00:58:57.550 --> 00:59:00.330
because they were at the bottom
and Stanford was at the top.

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:05.130
Stanford's best player, I don't know,
tore her ACL or something.

00:59:05.130 --> 00:59:08.150
Harvard won the game
and it was played at Stanford.

00:59:08.150 --> 00:59:09.470
Why?
Because they had teamwork.

00:59:09.470 --> 00:59:11.540
Ask their coach.

00:59:11.540 --> 00:59:14.510
They could, Harvard could
lose their best player.

00:59:14.510 --> 00:59:16.650
Stanford couldn't.

00:59:16.650 --> 00:59:18.870
- OK, let's take one last question here.

00:59:18.870 --> 00:59:19.700
Number two.

00:59:19.700 --> 00:59:22.120
- Hi, Melanie Mitchell,
Portland State University.

00:59:22.120 --> 00:59:24.310
- Hey.
- Hey.

00:59:24.310 --> 00:59:29.460
You made the really great suggestion
that products that have AI in them,

00:59:29.460 --> 00:59:30.720
like the Barbie example,

00:59:30.720 --> 00:59:34.260
should advertise
their limitations.

00:59:34.260 --> 00:59:35.990
- Yeah.
- But I'm wondering if you can comment

00:59:35.990 --> 00:59:38.940
a little bit more on how feasible that actually is,

00:59:38.940 --> 00:59:40.730
given that many of the systems

00:59:40.730 --> 00:59:43.150
that seem to be the most successful

00:59:43.150 --> 00:59:44.850
are the hardest to understand?

00:59:48.350 --> 00:59:55.540
- So first, let me say that a system
knowing its own limits

00:59:55.540 --> 00:59:57.560
is one of the hardest problems I know of.

00:59:59.030 --> 01:00:02.980
With respect to the Barbie doll,
I'd like to get to the first step

01:00:02.980 --> 01:00:06.740
of not being deceptive about
the capabilities that a system has.

01:00:06.740 --> 01:00:08.730
And the designers know.

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:11.930
So I didn't say this with Barbie,
but it's one of my quips about it.

01:00:12.780 --> 01:00:14.970
Most of us have known three-year-olds.

01:00:14.970 --> 01:00:18.510
I don't know a single three-year-old
who follows a script.

01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:21.210
I mean, does anybody?

01:00:21.210 --> 01:00:23.090
If you do, raise your hand,
because I want to talk to you.

01:00:24.450 --> 01:00:27.370
I know a lot of parents who would like
to know how this happened.

01:00:29.820 --> 01:00:30.800
They always, OK.

01:00:30.800 --> 01:00:32.200
We don't follow scripts.

01:00:32.200 --> 01:00:34.690
That's why some of these
chat bots have trouble.

01:00:34.690 --> 01:00:39.400
So I would just like them to say, look, this is a cute,
it's a, you know, for a normal kid

01:00:39.400 --> 01:00:40.880
in a healthy environment

01:00:40.880 --> 01:00:42.500
with great parents,
it probably doesn't matter.

01:00:42.500 --> 01:00:44.200
The kid will throw it away after two days.

01:00:46.200 --> 01:00:49.420
I would like to start with not
exaggerating the capabilities.

01:00:50.540 --> 01:00:51.210
- OK.

01:00:51.210 --> 01:00:53.790
Let's thank Barbara again
for her stimulating talk,

01:00:53.790 --> 01:01:01.560
and she'll be around
for the next few days.

